abstract
We have measured growth and respiration in black soldier fly (BSF) larvae fed with mixtures of a lowquality substrate (degassed sludge, DS), and a high-quality substrate (chicken feed, CF) in order to elucidate how substrate quality affect larval metabolism and feed conversion into new biomass. The BSF larvae grew faster and became larger the higher the content of CF was in the substrate. Growth followed a sigmoidal curve from where the specific growth rate was determined and compared to measured specific respiration rates, in order to estimate costs of growth, maintenance metabolism, rates of feed assimilation, and net growth efficiency. Specific feed assimilation rates were similar on all substrate mixtures. Maximal specific growth rates were also affected only little unless the larvae were grown in pure DS. In contrast, the cost of growth and the maintenance metabolism were larger the higher the proportion of DS was. High specific growth rates were, in addition, sustained for shorter periods of the time the more DS was included in the substrate mixtures. In effect, higher proportions of the assimilated feed were spent on respiratory purposes instead of being converted into larval biomass and the net growth efficiency decreased the more DS was included in the substrate mixtures. We conclude that substrate quality may affect the conversion of feed into new biomass via alterations of the metabolic performance of BSF larvae and thereby the overall performance of BSF larval cultures. Ó 2020 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.
1. Introduction
The black soldier fly (BSF), Hermetia illucens receives substantial attention for use in waste management, and for feed and food production (Gold et al., 2018; Bosch et al., 2019). BSF larvae have been grown on a variety of substrates, ranging from organic wastes and different types of sludge over various agricultural by-products to commercial animal feed blends (Diener et al., 2009; Oonincx et al., 2015; Rehman et al., 2017; Abduh et al, 2018; Liu et al., 2018; Meneguz et al., 2018; Ganda et al., 2019; Lalander et al., 2019; Palma et al., 2019; Gold et al., 2020). The efficiency by which BSF larvae utilise different substrates for growth is highly variable (Bosch et al., 2019) and may be related to the quality of the substrate. Earlier results have shown that degassed (digested) sludge from anaerobic digesters is an example of a low-quality substrate for BSF larvae (Lalander et al., 2019). Although rich in proteins and other constituents of nutritional value, growth of BSF larvae on degassed sludge is very slow, the larvae remain small and do not complete their development into prepupae, and feed conversion into new biomass is low (Lalander et al., 2019). Commercial
chicken feed blends are, on the other hand, excellent substrates for BSF larvae, supporting rapid growth and larval development (Diener et al., 2009; Liu et al., 2017a; Lalander et al., 2019). Some variations in the performance of BSF larvae across substrates have been explained by the ratio of proteins to carbohydrates (BarragánFonseca et al., 2019; Gold et al., 2020), quality of the macromolecular components (Barragán-Fonseca et al., 2018), moisture content (Cheng et al., 2017), and indigestible components (Gold et al., 2020). Feed conversion efficiency in BSF larvae is most often evaluated over the entire larval growth phase. BSF larvae have been able to convert 2–16% of different substrates into new biomass (Diener et al., 2009; Rehman et al., 2017; Abduh et al., 2018; Ganda et al., 2019; Lalander et al., 2019), 14–24% of ingested feed into new biomass (Oonincx et al., 2015), or 4–38% of digested feed into new biomass (Diener et al., 2009; Abduh et al., 2018; Liu et al., 2018; Meneguz et al., 2018; Palma et al., 2019; Gold et al., 2020) when measurements are based on dry weights (DW). Substrates for BSF larvae are moist and contain active microorganisms (Palma et al., 2019). At certain conditions, microbial substrate consumption may even exceed that of the larvae (Bekker et al., submitted for publication), and therefore feed conversion efficiency does not solely reflect the actual performance of the
