BSF larvae. More insight into the metabolic performance of BSF larvae is provided by the net growth efficiency, NGE (or net energy of growth), as this describes the ratio between biomass gain and feed assimilation (see e.g. Chaplin and Chaplin, 1981). Hence, NGE is an efficiency measure that takes only the feed components actually assimilated by the BSF larvae into consideration and defines the upper limit of feed conversion efficiency. Information on larval NGE across substrates of low and high quality will be highly useful for optimization of BSF substrate compositions and production processes and enable different wasteand by-products to be utilised most efficiently as feed for BSF larvae. The aim of this study is to quantify and compare the metabolic performance of BSF larvae growing on mixtures (0–100%) of a lowquality substrate (degassed sludge) and a high-quality substrate (commercial chicken feed) in terms of NGE, combined with estimates of costs of growth and maintenance, as well as feed assimilation, respiration, and growth. The purpose is to elucidate how and to which extent the metabolic performance of BSF larvae may be affected by the quality and the nature of the growth substrate, and how this translates into variability of NGE.
2. Methods
2.1. Cultivation of parental black soldier flies and nursery
Domesticated black soldier flies (BSF), Hermetia illucens were supplied by the Danish company, Entomass ApS, which is a commercial producer of BSF larvae. A breeding culture of BSF was established in the laboratory in a temperature-controlled chamber at 28 °C, a relative humidity of 67%, and a 12:12 h light:dark period. Larvae were reared on broiler chicken feed (CF) containing 70% water in a cylindrical bucket (diameter 30 cm, height 35 cm, initial depth of feed 10 cm). Additional CF was added regularly. From the 3rd generation of adult flies reared in the laboratory, 0.7 g eggs were harvested from an egg trap made of cardboard placed above a mixture of decaying banana and apple. The eggs were transferred to a nursery made from a cylindrical bucket (10 cm in diameter, 13 cm in height, initial depth of CF, 5 cm). After 10 days of incubation, the neonate larvae had reached a size where they could be counted and handled without damage.
2.2. Substrates
Substrates for the BSF larvae were prepared from mixtures (0100%) of degassed sludge (DS) collected at a local biogas facility (Nature Energy, Vaarst, Denmark) producing biogas from cattle and pig manure supplemented with industrial organic waste, and a commercial chicken feed (CF) blend (Paco Start 19, DLG, Denmark). Before use, DS was sieved through a 1 mm sieve to remove larger particles and subsequently centrifuged at 1000 g for 5 min. after which the supernatant was discharged in order to increase the dry matter content. Water was added until saturation after the substrates had been mixed. Lipid (measured by NMR), carbohydrate (measured as monosaccharides on an HPLC after enzymatic digestion), protein (measured by the Dumas method) and ash contents (measured gravimetrically after combustion in an ashing furnace) in DS and CF were quantified by an accredited laboratory for nutritional and chemical analyses of foods (ALS, Humlebæk, Denmark).
2.3. Cultivation of BSF larvae
BSF larvae were grown in cylindrical plastic buckets (10 cm in diameter, 13 cm in height) containing 50 g DW of watersaturated substrate mixtures. Larval growth on each substrate
mixture was examined in 4 replicate cultures. Fifty neonate larvae, 10 days of age, were distributed into each of the buckets on Day 0 of the cultivation trials. The buckets were incubated under the same conditions as the breeding culture. Each culture was supplemented with another 50 g (DW) of the same water-saturated substrate mixture as originally used on Day 6 (post inoculation) except for the cultures grown on pure sludge, which were supplemented with only 16.7 g (DW) of water-saturated DS. Prepupae were sampled from a petri dish placed on top of the substrate on a daily basis and stored frozen for further analysis of DW and organic content.
2.4. CO2 production rate
Respiration rates, quantified as the rate of CO2 production per individual, QCO2 were measured on a daily basis on 6 randomly selected larvae from each bucket. Measurements were carried out as quickly and gently as possible. Immediately after larvae had been collected, they were transferred to a 50 mL plastic centrifugation tube placed in an aluminium heating block (Hettich Benelux model MHR-23) at 28 °C. A pre-calibrated PS-3208 wireless CO2 Sensor (Pasco, Roseville, California) was inserted into the top of the tube and the CO2 concentration in the tube was recorded at a frequency of 1 Hz. The linear increase in CO2 concentration recorded for a period of 3 min, starting 2 min after the tube had been closed, was used as measure of QCO2.
2.5. Wet weight, dry weight and organic fraction
After CO2 production rates had been quantified, the wet weight of the 6 randomly selected larvae (including gut content) was measured on an electronic scale before they were returned alive to their original bucket. Dry weight (DW) was measured at 3-day intervals on 5 BSF larvae randomly collected from cultures grown under comparable conditions and in parallel to the cultures used for growth experiments, in the same substrate mixtures and under the same conditions. The larvae were killed by freezing and dried for 24 h at 105 °C. It was assumed that the dry matter content of larvae in the growth experiments resembled these measurements. Wet weight and dry weight were measured also on the prepupae collected on top of all cultures. The dried prepupae were then transferred to a furnace at 550 °C for 5 h and the weight of the remaining ash was measured. The mass difference between dry weight and ash was used to evaluate the organic fraction of the prepupae.
