2.9. Conversion factors
In order to compare measurements of growth and CO2 production, all measurements were expressed as specific rates based on the mass of carbon in the larvae and in CO2. The specific CO2
production rate, qCO2, was calculated by multiplying the larval CO2 production rate, QCO2 by the mass-fraction of carbon in CO2 and dividing by the mass of carbon in one larva. We assumed that carbon made up 49% of the organic fraction of the larvae, which is the carbon content in biomass of average elementary composition (Roels, 1980). The organic fraction of the larvae was estimated from measurements of the dry weight of the larvae and measurements of the organic fraction of prepupae reared on each substrate mixture. In order to express Y, m and NGE in terms of energy, we considered that the combustion heat of biomass of average elementary composition is 560 kJ C-mol 1, corresponding to 22.7 kJ g 1 (Roels, 1980). It was assumed, that also the feed components actually assimilated by the BSF larvae had an average elementary composition similar to the BSF larvae.
2.10. Statistical analysis
Results from 4 replicate cultures on all substrate mixtures were compared in terms of a null hypothesis of equal larval performance on all substrate mixtures. Larval performances were considered significantly different when the null hypothesis was rejected by ANOVA analysis and t-tests at 5% probability level.
3. Results
3.1. Growth and development
Chicken feed (CF) had the highest content of carbohydrates while degassed sludge (DS) had highest contents of proteins and inorganic components. Both substrates had low lipid contents (Table 1). The BSF larvae grew on both substrates and on mixtures of the two, though no larvae reached the prepupal stage within 30 days when grown solely on DS. The higher the content of CF in the substrate mixtures, the larger became the proportions of individuals that developed into prepupae (Fig. 1, left panels). With 25% CF in the substrate mixtures, 32% of the larvae developed into prepupae within 30 days, increasing to 80–89% on the substrate mixtures with highest CF contents (Table 1). DS also prolonged the larval stage of the BSF larvae. The first prepupae from larvae grown on 100% CF appeared on Day 13 (post inoculation) and half of the prepupae had formed on Day 17, 27 days of age. Larvae grown on 75% DS began to develop into prepupae on Day 17 and half of the prepupae had formed on Day 23, 33 days of age. Inclusion of 75% DS in the substrate mixtures thus prolonged larval development by 6 days. The dry weight content of the larvae increased during the growth phase (Supplementary information, Fig. S1) and the average dry weight content of the larvae and the organic fraction of the dry weight were highest in substrate mixtures rich in CF (Supplementary information, Fig. S2). The maximal size of larvae, Xmax and the maximal specific growth rate, lmax, estimated from Eq. (1) was only 6.9 mg and 0.22 day 1, respectively, for larvae grown on DS (Table 1). Larvae grew significantly larger on substrates containing CF and the higher the CF content, the larger became the larvae (p < 0.05). Xmax, was almost 3 times larger for larvae grown on pure CF (94 mg DW) compared to larvae grown on 75% DS (38 mg DW). In contrast, lmax were similar (0.54–0.60 day 1) for larvae grown on 0–75% DS (for larvae grown on 25–75% sludge, differences in lmax were not significant, p < 0.05). The specific growth rate remained close to its maximal value for about 7 days (14 days for larvae grown in DS), in which period growth was exponential (Supplementary information, Fig. S3). The growth rate of the larvae, rX (Eq. (2)) peaked on Day 8 on all substrate mixtures, except for 100% DS in which rX did not peak until Day 11 (Fig. 1, left panels). The period from
