3.2. Cost of growth and maintenance costs
The rate of CO2 production per larvae, QCO2 was maximal around at the same time as also rX was maximal (Fig. 1, left panels). In Fig. 1 (central panels) specific CO2 production rates, qCO2 are compared to specific growth rates, l estimated from Eq. (3). On all substrate mixtures did qCO2 depend on l, and the dependency was reasonably well described by a linear relationship as predicted by Eq. (5). The higher the CF content in mixed substrates, the higher was also the coefficient of determination, r2 derived from linear regression analysis (Table 1), indicating that Eq. (5) provided the best description of the actual relationship between qCO2 and l when the CF content was high. The higher the content of DS in the substrate mixtures, the higher became also the cost of growth, Y and maintenance requirement, m (p < 0.05). Highest Y and m were seen in larvae grown on DS while lowest Y and m were observed in larvae grown on CF. Particularly m was affected by the proportion of DS in the substrate mixtures, while Y was remarkably high when DS was the sole substrate.
3.3. Net growth efficiency
Fig. 2 shows how NGE depended on the specific growth rate, l on the different substrate mixtures. NGE depended strongly on l, particularly when l was lower than roughly 0.2 day 1. NGE also depended on the ratio between DS and CF irrespectively of how
rapidly the BSF larvae were growing. Fig. 1, right panels show l (Eq. (3)), specific CO2 production rate, qCO2 (Eq. (5)), specific assimilation rate, a (Eq. (4)), and NGE (Eq. (7)) against time of BSF larvae grown on the different substrate mixtures. All values were highest in small larvae. After Day 5, l, qCO2 and a began to decline. NGE remained close to its maximal value for another 3–5 days. The specific assimilation rate, a was almost the same across substrate mixtures the first days of the cultivations while NGE was considerably lower in larvae grown on DS compared to larvae fed also with CF. Maximal specific assimilation rate, amax, maximal specific CO2 production rate, qCO2,max, and maximal net growth efficiency, NGEmax were estimated from Eqs. (4), (5) and (8), respectively, using the maximal specific growth rates estimated for each substrate mixture (Table 1). Whereas amax was almost independent of substrate composition, qCO2,max was highest in larvae grown on 100% DS and lowest in larvae grown on 100% CF. Consequently, NGEmax was highest in larvae fed only with CF. Average net growth efficiencies, NGEavg evaluated from Day 0 until the first prepupae emerged showed that 48% of the assimilated feed was converted into biomass in the BSF larvae fed only with CF while just 9% of the assimilated feed ended up as biomass in larvae fed only with DS.
