absorbed feed were therefore lost via respiration and, as a result, the net growth efficiency, NGE became low on substrate mixtures rich in DS. The BSF cultures were in total supplied with 1.3 g (cultures grown solely on DS) or 2 g (remaining cultures) of dry substrate per larva, corresponding in amounts to 21–188 times the final dry weight of the larvae. Feed was therefore supplied in surplus and larval performances on the different mixtures of substrates can be compared based on the model outputs presented in Table 1. The reliability of these data depends on the ability of Eqs. (1) and (5) to actually describe growth and respiration. The growth experiments in Fig. 1 were started with 10 days old larvae and maximal sizes were obtained 12–15 days later. At this time, the mass of the larvae was 167–308 mg wet weight or 38–94 mg dry weight, except for larvae grown solely on DS. These results are in line with earlier studies, in which it has taken 15–40 days for BSF larvae to reach their maximal masses of 150–320 mg wet weight (Liu et al., 2017a; Barragán-Fonseca et al., 2018; Lalander et al., 2019). When the larvae formed prepupae they may have emptied their gut and started to rely solely on endogen metabolism, which is in line with the modest decreases in weight that occured (Fig. 1). Until then growth curves were accurately described by the logistic function (Eq. (1)), although it describes growth as a continuous process not taking e.g. the transition between instars into consideration. Feed uptake in BSF larvae is restricted to instar 1–6 during the sigmoidal growth phase (Gligorescu et al., 2019) and respiration rates were also seen to decrease as the larvae approached their maximal sizes (Fig. 1, left panels). During the growth phase, the specific CO2 production rate, qCO2 was close to linearly correlated to the specific growth rate, l as predicted by Eq. (5), in particular when the CF content was high. Therefore, it seems that balanced growth is most closely accommodated when BSF larvae are reared on a high-quality substrate. The increasing DW contents, predominantly after Day 9 (Supplementary information, Fig. S1), probably reflect that the lipid content increased when the larvae approached the prepupal stage. Most of the biomass was, however produced before this point (Fig. 1, left panels). The specific feed assimilation rate, a was almost the same on all substrate mixtures (Fig. 1, right panels and Table 1). Small BSF larvae assimilated a daily amount of feed corresponding roughly to their body size when measured in terms of carbon equivalents.
Unidentified compounds or conditions in the degassed sludge seemed to result in increased maintenance metabolism in the BSF larvae. The maintenance coefficient, m was 4 times larger in larvae grown only on DS compared to larvae grown only on CF (Table 1). The higher the content of DS in the substrate mixtures, the larger became the proportion of assimilated feed utilized for maintenance purposes and therefore unavailable for new biomass production. Since also the cost of growth, Y was higher on DS than on CF, NGE of BSF larvae grown on substrate mixtures rich in DS became low. Although the larval growth phase was extended by 6 days when the DS content was increased from 0% to 75%, this was insufficient to compensate for the decrease in growth rates. Prepupae produced on 75% DS ended up being only half the size of the prepupae produced on CF. The cost of growth in terms of energy ranging from 4.9 kJ g 1 DW on CF to 25.4 kJ g 1 DW on DS (Table 1) falls below and above, respectively, the cost of growth observed in pigs (11.4–14.4 kJ g 1 DW, van Milgen et al., 2001) and broiler chickens (10.4–11.9 kJ g 1 DW, Liu et al., 2017b). Specific maintenance requirements are expectedly related to the size of the animals (Riisgård, 1998). BSF larvae are comparatively small in size and their maintenance requirements of 0.9 kJ g 1 DW day 1 when grown on CF (Table 1) was also slightly higher than the 0.70.8 kJ g 1 DW day 1 that e.g. can be expected for a 0.1 kg broiler chicken (calculated from Liu et al., 2017b). The maintenance requirement of BSF larvae grown on DS (4.0 kJ g 1 DW day 1) was much higher than what is seen in chickens. It has been questioned if respiration rates measured in insects taken out of their normal living site and put into respiration chambers reflect the respiration rates of the undisturbed animals. Wightman (1981) found that so-called ‘flask effects’ resulted in respiration measurements below what should be expected from energy balances taking into account the ingested feed, the excreted faecal components, and growth. However, inconsistent energy balances may also be due e.g. to respiratory processes in some types of feed (McEvoy, 1985), and less than 10% discrepancy between predicted and measured respiration rates have been observed in insects reared in the laboratory. In this study, the larvae were gently taken out of their substrate immediately before respiration measurements, to avoid interference from microbial respiration, and transferred directly to the respiration chamber, in which air temperature was adjusted to 28 °C and at atmospheric CO2 partial pressure. The larvae spend only 5 min. in the respiration chamber to avoid starvation. CO2 concentrations increased steadily during the measurement periods and the measured respiration rates were similar between equal-sized larvae from replicate cultures. This indicates that sufficient precautions were taken in order to avoid the larvae being severely disturbed during measurements. The metabolic rates (Table 1) can also be compared to earlier measurements in BSF larvae. The maximal specific CO2 production rate qCO2,max for larvae grown on 100% CF corresponded to a specific power generation of 12.9 lW mg 1 WW while the maintenance coefficient, m of these larvae corresponded to a specific rate of heat production of 3 lW mg 1 WW. These values are in almost perfect agreement with previous measurements of metabolic activity in BSF larvae, quantified by calorimetry (Gligorescu et al., 2019). These authors found that the heat production decreased from 12 lW mg 1 of WW in rapidly growing BSF larvae below 10 mg WW in size to 2 lW mg 1 of wet weight in BSF larvae of 90 mg WW in stadium 6 when growth ceased. Such highly similar results suggest that the metabolic characteristics listed in Table 1 do represent general performances of BSF larvae reared on substrates of variable quality. The low NGE on DS was expected. In an earlier study did BSF larvae digest 13% of the DS used as substrate while only 0.2% was converted into new biomass (Lalander et al., 2019), corresponding to a NGE of 0.02 (based on dry weight). This value is even lower than
