the average net growth efficiency, NGEavg of 0.09 (based on carbon equivalents) found in this study (Table 1). The assimilated feed from the substrate mixtures containing CF was converted 2–5 times more efficiently into new biomass, with NGEavg values of 0.22–0.48. These values are well in line with previous studies, where 3.8–38% of the digested feed have been incorporated into new biomass (Diener et al., 2009; Abduh et al., 2018; Liu et al., 2018; Meneguz et al., 2018; Palma et al., 2019; Gold et al., 2020). The highest NGE value was found in small BSF larvae grown solely on CF. In these larvae, the maximal net growth efficiency, NGEmax attained a value of 0.63, which is comparable to what has been observed also in other insects (e.g. 0.44–0.89 in milkweed bugs, Chaplin and Chaplin, 1981) and invertebrates (e.g. 0.6 and 0.7 in the marine polychaetes Nereis diversicolor and N. virens, respectively, Nielsen et al., 1995). NGEmax marks the upper limit for feed conversion efficiency. Since NGEmax was lowered by DS (Table 1), also the potential for feed conversion into new biomass was reduced when DS was included in the substrate mixtures. None of the BSF cultures fulfilled their potential for feed conversion. The BSF larvae maintained high NGE for 8–10 days (Fig. 1, right panels) until they had gained 40–60% of their final weight. The remaining biomass was then produced at a NGE well below NGEmax and NGEavg was therefore lower than NGEmax on all substrate mixtures (Table 1). High specific growth rates were maintained for relatively short periods of time on the substrate mixtures rich in DS, and the difference between NGEavg and NGEmax was therefore highest when also the DS content was highest. BSF larvae grown solely on CF were reared with a NGEavg corresponding to 76% of NGEmax. BSF larvae grown solely on DS were reared with a NGEavg corresponding to only 45% of NGEmax. This means that there are two reasons for DS being a lower quality substrate than CF, both related to the metabolic performance of the BSF larvae: the potential for feed conversion into new biomass is substrate-specific and lowest on DS, and the BSF larvae meet their potential feed conversion to a lesser degree on DS.
5. Conclusion
The two substrates, degassed sludge (DS) and chicken feed (CF) strongly affected how assimilated feed was utilised by BSF larvae, causing large differences in feed conversion efficiency and growth. Although the larvae assimilated feed as rapidly from DS as from CF, they did not convert the feed from DS and CF into new biomass with the same efficiency. High rates of maintenance metabolism and increased cost of growth caused the larvae to utilise more feed for respiration purposes when DS was included in substrate mixtures, and less feed remained available for growth. DS also reduced the period of time the BSF larvae maintained growth at specific rates close to lmax, and this increased the contribution of maintenance to the overall metabolism. The interplay between substrate quality and metabolic performance, here elucidated by a highly variable, substrate dependent NGE, may therefore have considerable consequences for the efficiency by which BSF larvae convert different substrates into new biomass.
